meta {
  name: 30 - Initiatives Bare
  type: graphql
  seq: 30
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}
  body: graphql
  auth: inherit
}

body:graphql {
  query InitiativesBare {
    initiatives(first: 2, includeArchived: true, sort: [{updatedAt: {order: Ascending}}]) {
      nodes {
        id
        createdAt
        updatedAt
        archivedAt
        name
        description
        slugId
        icon
        color
        status
        priority
        sortOrder
        prioritySortOrder
        targetDate
        startedAt
        completedAt
        canceledAt
        healthUpdatedAt
        trashed
        url
        content
        creator { id }
        owner { id }
        leadTeam { id }
        parentInitiative { id }
        organization { id }
      }
      pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
    }
  }
}

docs {
  Reference: https://linear.app/developers/graphql

  The canonical Initiatives selection set — verbatim what the connector will send. Same
  construction rules as `10 - Issues Bare`. Nested connections (`projects`,
  `subInitiatives`, `initiativeUpdates`, `links`, `documents`, `history`, `labels`) are
  omitted; note `parentInitiative { id }` IS included, since initiative hierarchy is a
  scalar-cost relation and is the field a downstream consumer needs to rebuild the tree.

  Static cost under the documented formula (22 scalars, 5 relation objects): per node =
  1 + 22 x 0.1 + 5 x 1.1 = ~8.7 points; at `first: 250` that is ~2,175 points — the
  cheapest of the four streams.

  `initiatives` accepts the `sort` argument (verified by introspection), so this stream
  uses the ascending `updatedAt` sweep and may checkpoint per page.

  Note `Initiative.status` is an ENUM here (`InitiativeStatus`), NOT an object — unlike
  `Project.status`, which is a `ProjectStatus` OBJECT and must be selected as
  `status { id }`. The two streams look symmetrical but their `status` fields are not; a
  shared selection-set template that treated them identically would fail validation on one
  of them. This is exactly the kind of near-miss the per-stream explicit selection sets
  exist to keep honest.

  **Limitation / observed finding — `identifier` is ENTITLEMENT-GATED and has been REMOVED
  from this selection set.** With the stream empty (2026-07-30) the field resolved without
  complaint. Once `Seeding/A1`/`A2` created a real record (2026-08-04), requesting it
  returned:

      HTTP 200
      "errors": [{ "extensions": { "code": "INPUT_ERROR" },
                   "message": "Feature 'Initiative IDs' is not enabled",
                   "path": ["initiatives","nodes",0,"identifier"] }]

  **and `data` was still populated** — the row came back intact, with the errors array
  alongside it. This is the third and nastiest variant of the "HTTP 200 does not mean
  success" rule in this collection: not a total failure (`06`, `data: null`) and not a
  clean rejection (`42`, HTTP 400), but a PARTIAL SUCCESS carrying both real data and a
  real error.

  Two lessons carried into the plan:

  1. **Entitlement-gated fields are a portability hazard unique to GraphQL selection sets.**
     REST returns whatever the plan allows; GraphQL returns only what you ask for, so asking
     for a field the workspace is not entitled to poisons every response — on every page,
     forever. `identifier` is a paid add-on ('Initiative IDs') and is not worth that: `id`, `slugId`
     and `url` already identify the record. Removed.
  2. **The connector's error handling must distinguish partial success from total failure.**
     Treating any non-empty `errors` array as fatal would break the sync outright on every
     workspace lacking this feature; ignoring `errors` would hide genuine failures. Branch
     on whether `data` is present.

  Only discoverable with data in the stream — the empty-endpoint run gave a false all-clear.

  **Limitation / observed finding — POPULATED as of 2026-08-04.** `Seeding/A2` created one
  initiative, and it succeeded — which also settles the entitlement question below:
  **Initiatives ARE enabled on this workspace**, so no graceful-degradation path is needed.
  The saved example confirms `status` resolves as a PLAIN STRING (`"Active"`), in contrast to
  `Project.status`'s object — exactly the asymmetry flagged above, now evidenced.
  `identifier` was stripped for the entitlement reason above.

  <!-- Historical, pre-seed (2026-07-30): -->
  **Superseded — this endpoint WAS empty.** The Estuary workspace contained ZERO
  initiatives, with or without `includeArchived: true` (confirmed 2026-07-30). Initiatives
  are additionally a paid-plan feature, so an empty result may reflect plan entitlement
  rather than merely unpopulated data — worth confirming before treating the emptiness as
  benign. The query IS accepted and all fields resolve, proving the selection set is valid
  against the live schema, but no response body has ever been observed.

  Closing this gap requires `Seeding/A2 - Create Initiative`, which the user must run.
}

example {
  name: 200 — populated Initiative (status is a STRING, not an object)

  request: {
    url: https://api.linear.app/graphql
    method: POST
    mode: graphql

    body:graphql: {
      query InitiativesBare {
        initiatives(first: 2, includeArchived: true, sort: [{updatedAt: {order: Ascending}}]) {
          nodes {
            id
            createdAt
            updatedAt
            archivedAt
            name
            description
            slugId
            icon
            color
            status
            priority
            sortOrder
            prioritySortOrder
            targetDate
            startedAt
            completedAt
            canceledAt
            healthUpdatedAt
            trashed
            url
            content
            creator { id }
            owner { id }
            leadTeam { id }
            parentInitiative { id }
            organization { id }
          }
          pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  response: {
    headers: {
      x-complexity: 15
      x-ratelimit-requests-limit: 2500
      x-ratelimit-requests-remaining: 2483
      x-ratelimit-complexity-limit: 3000000
      x-ratelimit-complexity-remaining: 2999985
    }

    status: {
      code: 200
      text: OK
    }

    body: {
      type: json
      content: '''
        {
          "data": {
            "initiatives": {
              "nodes": [
                {
                  "id": "44f4e104-762d-43b3-a154-78fa72567bdb",
                  "createdAt": "2026-08-04T18:35:04.260Z",
                  "updatedAt": "2026-08-04T18:35:04.260Z",
                  "archivedAt": null,
                  "name": "estuary-connector-verification",
                  "description": "Temporary record created to verify source-linear Initiatives stream. Safe to delete.",
                  "slugId": "ba21ab221cce",
                  "icon": null,
                  "color": "#95a2b3",
                  "status": "Active",
                  "priority": 0,
                  "sortOrder": 0,
                  "prioritySortOrder": 0,
                  "targetDate": null,
                  "startedAt": null,
                  "completedAt": null,
                  "canceledAt": null,
